---
// "How the grades work": a short, collapsible explanation shown on every Draft view (Season Draft tab,
// Owner Draft tab, /draft). Wording follows GLOSSARY.md (Season points, Positional finish, Replacement
// level, Points over expected, Value over slot, Draft grade). `bestDrafters` adds the lines for the
// /draft page's all-time lists and Best drafters table.
interface Props { bestDrafters?: boolean }
const { bestDrafters = false } = Astro.props;
---
<details data-testid="draft-explainer" class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 px-4 py-3 text-sm">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none font-semibold text-paper marker:hidden">
    <span class="mr-1 inline-block text-muted transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
    How the grades work
  </summary>
  <dl class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-muted">
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Season points</dt>
      <dd>A player's fantasy points for the whole NFL regular season under that Season's scoring, as ESPN totals them. From 2021 that includes NFL week 18, after our fantasy season ends.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Positional finish (e.g. RB3)</dt>
      <dd>Where the player ranked by Season points among everyone at that position that Season, drafted or not.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Replacement level</dt>
      <dd>
        The last starter-quality player at a position: teams × starting slots, with each FLEX counted as half
        an RB and half a WR. In a 12-team league with 2 RB and 2 FLEX that's RB36. Points above replacement
        is a player's Season points minus that player's, and never below 0: anyone under it was waiver-wire
        level. Comparing with a replacement keeps QBs, kickers and defenses, who all score a lot, on the same
        scale as everyone else.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Points over expected</dt>
      <dd>
        Points above replacement minus what a pick in that round usually returns (the average across every
        Season). A 3rd-rounder who finishes WR2 is about <span class="font-semibold text-paper">+140</span>;
        a 1st-rounder who finishes below replacement is about <span class="font-semibold text-paper">−100</span>.
        This is what the numbers on each pick show and what the grades add up. Picks that weren't recorded
        aren't graded.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Draft grade</dt>
      <dd>The sum of a team's Points over expected for the Season, ranked against the rest of the league that Season. Ties share a rank.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Colors</dt>
      <dd>Green beat its round, magenta fell short; the stronger the color, the bigger the gap. The scale tops out at that Season's 90th percentile, so one huge hit doesn't wash out the rest.</dd>
    </div>
    {bestDrafters && (
      <>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Value over slot</dt>
          <dd>
            Ranks the Biggest steals. It compares a pick only with picks at the same position: where the player
            was taken among them, minus where they finished among the drafted players at that position. The
            60th WR taken who finishes 8th among drafted WRs is <span class="font-semibold text-paper">+52</span>.
            It rewards late-round finds, so it isn't used for the grades.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-semibold text-paper">Best drafters</dt>
          <dd>Each Owner's average Draft grade rank across every Season they drafted in. Lower is better.</dd>
        </div>
      </>
    )}
  </dl>
</details>
